t early when firms  hire someone from abroad it's either  because you're a recent graduate or a  designer or you have a very specialized  skill  and the further you get into your career  and you know once you become an  architect your experience gets more  specific to the region you're working in  you know in terms of the building code  or like construction methods and  you know most firms are going to be  hiring you to manage a project when  you're an architect and you can kind of  only do that if you have local  experience so  i would say  try to do this during your internship  and i think that will be the best time  for you to get that  international experience and get your  foot in the door in that region do you  find your job fulfilling and do you  specialize in a type of architecture if  so which ones so right now i do find the  job fulfilling but it wasn't always like  this i actually spent some time to think  about this because we have a couple new  interns in our office and i want to help  them you know get to a point where they  feel like this as well because the first  few years of my internship was kind of  hard for me  and i didn't really enjoy it that much  and  i think i  really only started to enjoy the work  and feeling fulfilled  when i had ownership of the project  instead of just doing a task and when i  started managing the project  or when i became the point of contact or  when i started controlling the output  that's when it became fun for me because  now i could actually see how the pieces  fit together and i could actually see  how the decisions that i made in the  beginning are impacting me during  construction and i think just my  personality type is  i think i'd like i want to say problem  solving  but maybe i just like talking to people  so  yeah i like working with different  clients and consultants and the other  part that's been fulfilling is like  helping the new interns and this is  actually the first year when i  started having someone help me and you  know usually i was the help frame like  the process of teaching him and like  figuring out the most effective way of  helping him and helping him learn i  think that's been pretty rewarding as  well and to answer the question of  do i specialize in any type of  architecture um i don't really  specialize in one type but i have done  quite a few small interior fit outs and  i'm also working on a library and my  thesis project was a library and i think  libraries are just so cool and i would  love to keep doing more of this so this  is a long question but i think it's  important that i read out the entire  question i understand these types of  injustices have been happening but see  the political social and economic events  and issues unfold at a global scale  especially in 2020 i feel as though  architecture does not have much of an  impact to help aid and solve some of  these issues considering who can afford  architectural services or design in  general even with good intentions of  solving or bringing awareness to  political social economic issues through  our work it is still up to the client at  the end of the day if these things are  in line with their needs and wants  understanding that impactful change can  occur more at an urban planning level  through establishing policies and etc  than architecture what then can  architecture truly impact at an everyday  scale is architecture's impact different  in that it exists only at a  phenomenological level this is a great  question julian and i used to think a  lot about this too one of the big things  that i've been learning a lot about is  creating an accessible and equitable  space for everyone you know a place  where you feel welcome no matter what  age or ethnicity or gender or you know  whether you're in a wheelchair or your  hearing impaired and  i didn't really learn about this in  school but now cities are really pushing  for a more accessible space for everyone  especially in the public realm in the  public project you know like libraries  or community centers it also depends on  what type of architecture you want to go  into architecture is so broad and you  really have to design your own career  you know some architects are not  interested in this you know like you  said they might be more interested in  construction or phenomenology  and maybe for this  a smaller scale like a residential  architecture  might be better suited for you but if  these social issues are high on the  priority list for you i would recommend  that you go to a firm that does more  institutional projects and not so much  private projects i think the fact that  you guys are asking these questions  and  really wanting to make a difference is  really cool  and  don't let this die out when you start  working and i think it's a part of your  job to protect your optimism and not get  so jaded by the realities of  construction i think the architecture  industry has been kind of stale for a  little while but right now i think the  environment is kind of ripe for some big  changes in the industry i don't know how  far along in school you are but when you  get into the field learn and absorb all  the information but don't take the  status quo as your standard you know  things that i'm doing at my job i  personally find this rewarding but you  guys are gonna be smarter and you're  gonna be more connected than me and so  you know you guys can do a lot more yeah  like i wouldn't take the architecture  industry as a standard and you know  don't accept my standard of fulfillment  as your standard and you know pave your  own way and  change the world i have a question about  your career  if one day your youtube career outrank  your architecture career i mean from  money clients fulfillment freedom and  even pressure would you continue to be  an architect or be a full-time youtuber  i feel all the architecture youtubers  struggle to be consistent including you  i think the short answer i